{%- doc -%}
  Gives Horizon's native product card (snippets/product-card.liquid, rendered on the home page,
  collection, search, cart, 404 and product-recommendation surfaces) its Mionas appearance.

  This is now the only product card in the theme. It used to be one of two: the home page composed
  blocks/_mionas-product-card.liquid and this file made the native card match it. The home page moved
  onto the native card, so there is nothing left to match -- these rules ARE the card's look, not a
  copy of one.

  _mionas-product-card.liquid has since been DELETED, along with mionas-product-media.liquid and
  mionas-product-title.liquid, once nothing rendered them. The declarations each rule below was
  derived from are therefore quoted inline rather than cited by line number: there is no longer a
  file to point at, and a citation into a deleted file is worse than none. Git history has the
  originals if the derivation is ever in question.

  Component-scoped bridge in the shape of snippets/header-bridge.liquid: only the properties Horizon
  exposes NO setting for live here. Everything settable -- background, border, radius, gap, padding,
  text colour -- is configured per card block in templates/*.json instead, so the theme editor stays
  the single source of truth for it and CSS never shadows a merchant control.

  Rendered from the end of snippets/design-system-bridge.liquid (Mionas-owned, itself rendered from
  layout/theme.liquid), which puts it after theme-styles-variables and after base.css. Hooking in
  there rather than in theme.liquid keeps every native .liquid file byte-identical to upstream
  Shopify/horizon, so merges stay clean.

  NOT COVERED: blocks/product-card.liquid, the public standalone "Product card" block. It has no
  instances in any template today. The rules below would apply to it, but its chrome comes from its
  own block settings -- a future merchant placement needs the templates/*.json values applied by hand
  or it renders shadow-and-600-weight on a borderless, radius-0 box.

  Deliberately left alone: quick-add button, badges, swatches, carousel arrows, predictive search.

  @example
  {% render 'product-card-bridge' %}
{%- enddoc -%}

{% style %}
  /* Mionas cards carry a shadow; Horizon's card declares none (base.css:101-118 only *transitions*
     box-shadow, for the lift/scale hover effects). No native rule to outrank, so a plain declaration
     is enough. It has to sit on __content rather than on <product-card>, because __content is the
     element that border-override paints the border and radius onto. */
  .product-card .product-card__content {
    box-shadow: var(--card-base-shadow);
  }

  /* Mirrors the Mionas card's pressed state, which was
     `.mionas-product-card:active { opacity: var(--card-state-pressed-opacity); }`.
     Same reason as above for targeting __content: the opacity has to fade the bordered surface, not
     a wrapper that has no paint of its own. */
  .product-card:active .product-card__content {
    opacity: var(--card-state-pressed-opacity);
  }

  /* The Mionas card declared `font-family: var(--card-base-font-family);` on its root. The
     design-system bridge already points --font-family-archivo at the theme's loaded body face, so
     this inherits a real font rather than the DS primitive. */
  .product-card {
    font-family: var(--card-base-font-family);
  }

  /* NO MEDIA-INSET RULE HERE, ON PURPOSE -- and this is a reversal worth recording.

     The design system insets the card's media from the card's edges
     (ProductCard.module.css's .media spends --product-card-base-media-inset and
     --product-card-base-media-radius). This theme briefly followed that: a
     `.product-card .card-gallery { margin: var(--product-card-base-media-inset); }` rule here, plus
     border_radius: 12 on the _product-card-gallery block in every templates/*.json.

     Both were reverted. The store wants the product image flush to the card's edges, so the card's
     own 16px radius clips the image's top corners and nothing else rounds it. Leaving the gallery's
     12px radius on while flush would have shown card background in the top-corner notches between
     the image's 12px curve and the card's 16px one, and rounded the image's bottom corners against
     nothing mid-card.

     So --product-card-base-media-inset and --product-card-base-media-radius are deliberately
     unspent by this theme. That is a knowing divergence from the design system, not an oversight or
     a sync that was never done -- do not "fix" it by reintroducing the margin without also taking
     the gallery radius back to 12 everywhere. */

  /* NO MEDIA-HEIGHT RULE HERE, ON PURPOSE.
     Framing the media by height rather than ratio does not survive contact with the real markup:
     .product-media-container is not a child of .card-gallery (an <a class="contents"> sits between
     them, and with more than one image a whole slideshow-component does too,
     card-gallery.liquid:112-200), and --gallery-aspect-ratio is written *inline* on .card-gallery
     (card-gallery.liquid:72), so a stylesheet cannot outrank it on that element.

     The image_ratio setting is the supported lever, so every templates/*.json uses "square". At the
     card's real layout -- 4 columns at page width on the home page (templates/index.json), ~4 at
     product_card_size medium on collection -- square is within a few percent of the 200px the design
     system's ProductCard frames at, and it keeps the merchant control meaningful. At 4 columns the
     card is roughly 290px wide, and since the media is flush (no inset, see above) the media is that
     width too. Revisit only if the design system tokenizes a ratio.

     --product-media-fit already defaults to cover inline (card-gallery.liquid:115), so no rule is
     needed for that either. */

  /* Hover zoom. Scales the <img>, NOT .card-gallery: the gallery is the overflow-hidden frame, so
     scaling it would grow the card instead of zooming inside it. The global card_hover_effect
     setting stays "none" for the same reason -- its subtle-zoom variant scales the whole gallery box.

     Gated exactly as base.css:124-150 gates Horizon's own hover effects.

     Originally copied from blocks/mionas-product-media.liquid, which has since been deleted -- so
     this is not a duplicate kept in step with something else, it is the only hover zoom the card
     has. Removing it removes the behaviour. The scale and duration are still local custom properties
     rather than design tokens, because the design system has not tokenized them; promote both if it
     ever does. */
  .product-card .card-gallery {
    --product-card-media-hover-scale: 1.05;
    --product-card-media-hover-duration: 0.5s;
  }

  @media (any-pointer: fine) and (prefers-reduced-motion: no-preference) {
    .product-card .card-gallery img {
      transition: transform var(--product-card-media-hover-duration) ease;
    }

    .product-card:hover .card-gallery img {
      transform: scale(var(--product-card-media-hover-scale));
    }
  }

  /* Title and price weight. Both render through snippets/text.liquid as
     `.text-block.<type_preset>` -- title "paragraph", price "h6" per templates/*.json. Horizon has
     no weight setting for them (weight is derived from whichever font picker the block points at),
     but base.css:673 and base.css:814 declare it as font-weight: var(--font-paragraph--weight) /
     var(--font-h6--weight). So this is a variable re-point and wins regardless of stylesheet order,
     which matters because per-block stylesheet tags bundle later in the body.

     INVARIANT, now split across the two halves. --font-paragraph--weight still governs the native
     product-title block, which every surface renders, so that half tracks all of templates/*.json.
     --font-h6--weight governs the native price block, which only search.json, cart.json and 404.json
     still render -- index.json and collection.json use blocks/mionas-price.liquid, which sets its own
     weight from --product-card-price-font-weight (mionas-price.liquid:94-99) and emits no .text-block
     for this rule to reach. Both halves stay: if a card's title or price is ever switched to another
     type_preset, add the matching --font-<preset>--weight or the text silently falls back to the
     theme's font weight.

     Both tokens are 600, so one rule covers title and price. Colour is a real Horizon setting
     (text_color) and lives in templates/*.json, not here. */
  .product-card .text-block {
    --font-paragraph--weight: var(--card-title-font-weight);
    --font-h6--weight: var(--product-card-price-font-weight);
  }
{% endstyle %}
